The  utility  generates a formatted message catalog from stdin or
one or more message source text files The file is created  if  it
does not already exist.  If does exist, its messages are included
in  the new The new message text defined in replaces the old mes‐
sage text currently in when the set and  message  numbers  match.
The  generated message catalog contains message strings that will
be retrieved using the library call.  These messages are  dynami‐
cally  loaded by the Native Language Support (NLS) library at run
time.  Error messages are grouped into sets, and  a  program  can
load  a  particular  set depending on which type, or language, of
messages is desired.  The message  text  source  files  are  text
files  in  the format described below.  Note that the fields of a
message text source line are separated by space  or  tab  charac‐
ters.   Determines  the  set identifier to be used for all subse‐
quent messages until the next or end‐of‐file.   The  is  the  set
identifier  which is defined as a number in the range Set identi‐
fiers within a single source file need not  be  contiguous.   Any
string  following the set identifier is treated as a comment.  If
no directive is specified in a message text source file, all mes‐
sages will be located in the default message set Removes  message
set  from the catalog.  The is a set identifier in the range If a
